2\. I pass to the second, the #emph[excellence] of the privilege: #emph[All things work together for good.] This is a #emph[Jacob’s] staff in the hand of faith, with which we may walk cheerfully to the mountain of God. What will satisfy or content us, if this will not? #emph[All things work together for good.] This word συνεργεῖ, #emph[work together,] is a medical expression. Several poisonous ingredients put together, when they are tempered by the skill of the apothecary, make a sovereign medicine and work together for the good of the patient#footnote[#emph[Venena ex corporibus per venenata pharmaca educunt medici.] \[Physicians draw poisons out of bodies by poisonous medicines.\] Pet. Mart.]. So all God’s providences, being divinely tempered and sanctified, work together for the best for the saints. He who #emph[loves God] and is #emph[called according to His purpose] may say to his soul, “Take it easy,” for “you have plenty of good things laid up”: everything in the world will be for your good. This is a Christian’s #emph[cordial,] which may bring the color back into his face and make him like #emph[Jonathan,] who tasted the #emph[honey] at the end of the staff, “and his eyes brightened” (1 Sam 14:27). Why should a Christian disembowel himself? Why should he kill himself with worry, when all things will sweetly agree, yes, conspire together, for his good? The sum of the text is this:

#emph[Doctrine.] That all the various dealings of God with his children,#footnote[Doctrine.] by a special providence, turn to their good#footnote[Nemo qui sit Christianus de rerum catastrophe debet ambigere. \[No one who is a Christian ought to be in doubt about the outcome of things.\] #emph[P. Mart.]].

Ps 25:10: “All the LORD’s paths are mercy to those who keep His covenant.” If every path has mercy in it, then it works for good.

